1
00:00:02,403 --> 00:00:11,671
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have effort that results in pleasure, then you have a type of effort that results in pain, then you have the type of effort that's sort of neutral.

2
00:00:14,092 --> 00:00:14,273
Yeah.

3
00:00:15,514 --> 00:00:26,923
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you depend on effort per se, as in you depend on needing, having something to do, you will always then value effort that results in pleasure more.

4
00:00:30,412 --> 00:00:32,173
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you will always value sensuality.

5
00:00:32,993 --> 00:00:41,976
[SPEAKER_01]: And from that point of view, of being fully dependent upon having something to do, effort that results in pleasure seems effortless.

6
00:00:45,357 --> 00:00:47,578
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is a reward at the end of it, basically.

7
00:00:50,759 --> 00:00:57,482
[SPEAKER_01]: But what you want to, as I said before, what you want to learn is to stop being dependent upon needing something to do.

8
00:01:01,479 --> 00:01:07,321
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's something you have to be, like, factoring into the environment you choose to live in.

9
00:01:07,401 --> 00:01:12,443
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you do your duties, like in this case, here, you walk Pindapad.

10
00:01:12,463 --> 00:01:13,064
[SPEAKER_01]: That's about it.

11
00:01:13,164 --> 00:01:14,364
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the only duty you have.

12
00:01:15,565 --> 00:01:22,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, if you have duties arising around your kuti, you have to sweep the leaves, you have to repair the roof, that's also fine, because it's done for the practical purpose.

13
00:01:23,628 --> 00:01:25,849
[SPEAKER_01]: But if your mind is just finding things to do,

14
00:01:26,910 --> 00:01:29,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Because without having anything to do, it feels like it's sinking.

15
00:01:29,992 --> 00:01:31,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's not a valid reason.

16
00:01:31,333 --> 00:01:32,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, that's the reason.

17
00:01:32,294 --> 00:01:33,855
[SPEAKER_01]: The reason is distraction.

18
00:01:34,335 --> 00:01:37,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you do things that are practical, you're doing them because you want to distract yourself.

19
00:01:38,338 --> 00:01:41,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you can't expect a clear-cut imperfection immediately.

20
00:01:43,008 --> 00:01:50,738
[SPEAKER_01]: But you want to become... you want to start becoming aware of that background motivation in regard to your choices and actions.

21
00:01:51,259 --> 00:01:59,269
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why the Buddha said that the proper basis for your meditation is abiding in non-activity.

22
00:01:59,289 --> 00:02:00,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Like jhānas, basically.

23
00:02:01,618 --> 00:02:22,322
[SPEAKER_01]: And by that, it's not just, eh... you don't do things like work, talk, eh... and so on, duties around your kuti, but you stop doing including, like, things like reading, you stop doing your meditation techniques, inverted commas, you stop watching your breath, you stop being mindful, you stop doing everything.

24
00:02:23,902 --> 00:02:27,383
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, that results in the natural mindfulness of what's already there.

25
00:02:28,284 --> 00:02:31,506
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your thoughts will be arising, but you are not actively thinking.

26
00:02:31,987 --> 00:02:35,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Your body will be breathing, but you are not actively breathing.

27
00:02:36,570 --> 00:02:49,479
[SPEAKER_01]: By being aware of the non-activity aspect within these activities, means basically not appropriating these activities, not making them yours, means being mindful of them peripherally, without being engaged with them.

28
00:02:49,599 --> 00:02:52,521
[SPEAKER_01]: That means abiding on activity, and that's very pleasant, actually.

29
00:02:53,403 --> 00:03:03,285
[SPEAKER_01]: If you cultivate abiding in non-activity, you will stop valuing the pleasure that results out of effort, such as sensuality.

30
00:03:04,486 --> 00:03:05,186
[SPEAKER_01]: It's too coarse.

31
00:03:06,526 --> 00:03:17,869
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that you have to make the effort in the first place, means no amount of pleasure at the end of it will justify it, from the point of view of non-activity, non-disturbance, non-pleasure.

32
00:03:18,349 --> 00:03:22,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean you run away, you lock yourself, you don't talk to anyone, you avoid your duties.

33
00:03:23,499 --> 00:03:23,619
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

34
00:03:23,639 --> 00:03:36,791
[SPEAKER_01]: It means that you do the duties for the right reasons, and when those reasons are depleted, as in practical purpose is finished, the roof is repaired, you don't go talking to people, you don't go mixing, you don't find other things you need to do.

35
00:03:37,252 --> 00:03:43,217
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, if you do, you start becoming aware that you are actually doing it for the reasons of distraction.

36
00:03:45,659 --> 00:03:47,561
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, all you have to do is nothing.

37
00:03:49,368 --> 00:04:01,611
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's something people would struggle, because even the people who stop doing vayuga, stop... stop... stop distracting themselves in coarser terms, they would go to remote dwellings and such, and then they would be doing their meditation.

38
00:04:02,831 --> 00:04:04,831
[SPEAKER_01]: They would be doing their practice.

39
00:04:05,851 --> 00:04:11,973
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they just replaced coarse doing with more refined doing, but they're certainly still active.

40
00:04:14,613 --> 00:04:18,014
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is sit down, let your mind think, let your body breathe,

41
00:04:20,095 --> 00:04:25,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't lose the sight of it, but certainly don't try to actively control it, and don't try to do it.

42
00:04:27,722 --> 00:04:28,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything.

43
00:04:28,663 --> 00:04:33,948
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I must now, because I finished the work, I'm sitting here, I must then now practice the peripheral awareness and mindfulness.

44
00:04:35,109 --> 00:04:37,892
[SPEAKER_01]: You must discern it, but you must not do it.

45
00:04:40,874 --> 00:04:44,598
[SPEAKER_00]: I almost never really, like, do techniques, but, eh...

46
00:04:45,157 --> 00:04:50,078
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I gave that as an example to basically other people who might be listening to this talk, because that is quite common.

47
00:04:50,818 --> 00:04:54,319
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, and even if you don't define it as a technique, you would be doing something.

48
00:04:54,719 --> 00:04:54,859
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

49
00:04:55,079 --> 00:04:56,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'm reading the suttas, I'm trying to understand.

50
00:04:56,899 --> 00:04:57,519
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's good.

51
00:04:58,460 --> 00:05:04,861
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is also a time and place where you should stop doing even that, because you recognize even that it's a good activity.

52
00:05:04,901 --> 00:05:09,022
[SPEAKER_01]: It's activity that can result in understanding, you know, pondering on the meaning of the suttas.

53
00:05:09,982 --> 00:05:14,783
[SPEAKER_01]: But even that in itself, as the Buddha said, overly doing it,

54
00:05:16,893 --> 00:05:24,059
[SPEAKER_01]: It can result in disturbing the mind and then the mind will go too much the other way.

55
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:42,133
[SPEAKER_00]: I noticed something that, like, when a thing is arisen, a perception, let's say a noise outside, I have fear.

56
00:05:44,655 --> 00:05:45,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Have fear?

57
00:05:45,416 --> 00:05:45,596
[SPEAKER_00]: What?

58
00:05:46,453 --> 00:05:47,293
[SPEAKER_00]: Fear is the reason.

59
00:05:48,554 --> 00:05:49,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Based on the noise outside?

60
00:05:49,775 --> 00:05:51,516
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right, okay.

61
00:05:51,776 --> 00:06:01,841
[SPEAKER_00]: So, without trying to avoid that fear, I would, like, go on to abandon, sort of, like, grief in regards to that.

62
00:06:02,382 --> 00:06:03,102
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you mean grief?

63
00:06:03,302 --> 00:06:06,424
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, delighting in that notion of fear.

64
00:06:07,204 --> 00:06:07,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's not grief.

65
00:06:11,126 --> 00:06:11,447
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not?

66
00:06:14,128 --> 00:06:15,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, grief is grief, basically.

67
00:06:16,842 --> 00:06:17,663
[SPEAKER_01]: Grief is unpleasant.

68
00:06:17,683 --> 00:06:18,663
[SPEAKER_01]: Grief is a regret.

69
00:06:18,743 --> 00:06:20,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Grief is all those things.

70
00:06:20,885 --> 00:06:23,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but fear is that sort of thing, right?

71
00:06:23,166 --> 00:06:23,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

72
00:06:23,466 --> 00:06:23,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Fear?

73
00:06:24,267 --> 00:06:27,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Fear would be... Well, you're not... you're not happy about your fear.

74
00:06:27,249 --> 00:06:27,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

75
00:06:27,509 --> 00:06:28,509
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to get rid of it.

76
00:06:30,110 --> 00:06:30,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

77
00:06:30,871 --> 00:06:31,111
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

78
00:06:31,992 --> 00:06:32,272
[SPEAKER_00]: So that...

79
00:06:32,292 --> 00:06:33,492
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not grieving over it.

80
00:06:34,493 --> 00:06:37,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Wanting... wanting to get rid of it.

81
00:06:37,435 --> 00:06:37,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I see.

82
00:06:37,975 --> 00:06:38,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

83
00:06:38,416 --> 00:06:38,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is it?

84
00:06:38,776 --> 00:06:39,076
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

85
00:06:39,136 --> 00:06:39,336
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

86
00:06:39,416 --> 00:06:39,676
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

87
00:06:39,896 --> 00:06:40,777
[SPEAKER_01]: On that subtle level.

88
00:06:41,117 --> 00:06:41,357
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

89
00:06:41,878 --> 00:06:42,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

90
00:06:42,378 --> 00:06:42,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah.

91
00:06:42,778 --> 00:06:42,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

92
00:06:42,898 --> 00:06:42,998
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

93
00:06:43,018 --> 00:06:44,099
[SPEAKER_01]: You're trying to get rid of it.

94
00:06:44,820 --> 00:06:45,360
[SPEAKER_00]: But grief...

95
00:06:45,762 --> 00:06:50,383
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a bit sort of too coarse...

96
00:06:50,403 --> 00:06:50,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

97
00:06:50,623 --> 00:06:51,763
[SPEAKER_01]: ...to describe that.

98
00:06:52,944 --> 00:06:53,904
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you describe it?

99
00:06:53,924 --> 00:06:54,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Aversion.

100
00:06:55,084 --> 00:06:58,165
[SPEAKER_01]: You have aversion towards the arising experience, hence you try to get rid of it.

101
00:06:58,745 --> 00:06:59,325
[SPEAKER_01]: You reverse.

102
00:06:59,505 --> 00:07:02,466
[SPEAKER_01]: You reverse towards its arising, towards its manifestation.

103
00:07:03,966 --> 00:07:04,206
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

104
00:07:06,347 --> 00:07:08,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Resisting.

105
00:07:08,907 --> 00:07:09,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Yuri.

106
00:07:10,007 --> 00:07:10,427
[SPEAKER_01]: Yuri.

107
00:07:11,188 --> 00:07:11,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Get back here.

108
00:07:12,828 --> 00:07:13,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Sit.

109
00:07:16,234 --> 00:07:18,836
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you reverse towards fear, you're trying to get rid of it.

110
00:07:21,717 --> 00:07:21,937
Yeah.

111
00:07:23,538 --> 00:07:24,018
[SPEAKER_00]: So, go on.

112
00:07:24,238 --> 00:07:24,979
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you want to say?

113
00:07:25,339 --> 00:07:43,348
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, when the fear arises, abandoning, like, sort of like, carrying that forth, like, I would usually see it as a delightment of that fear.

114
00:07:44,772 --> 00:07:56,494
[SPEAKER_00]: Fear itself is sort of like... yeah, it does... it is unpleasant, but at the same time, you do dwell on it.

115
00:07:58,407 --> 00:08:00,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, you dwell on another habit of trying to get rid of it.

116
00:08:01,229 --> 00:08:06,313
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you want to stop doing, in that case, is not trying to get rid of the fear, or not try to get rid of the fear.

117
00:08:06,733 --> 00:08:13,277
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to stop entertaining the habit of aversion towards something that's disagreeable.

118
00:08:13,998 --> 00:08:15,099
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you want to stop doing.

119
00:08:16,019 --> 00:08:19,702
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to start seeing that fear has nothing to do with it directly.

120
00:08:22,507 --> 00:08:28,648
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but like, what about the perception of abandonment at that moment?

121
00:08:28,748 --> 00:08:39,971
[SPEAKER_00]: I've noticed that it works in the sense that that perception is there, that fear is still persisting, but my... No.

122
00:08:40,931 --> 00:08:41,771
[SPEAKER_01]: I know where you're going with this.

123
00:08:41,931 --> 00:08:44,552
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, yeah, perception is still there, fear is there.

124
00:08:44,792 --> 00:08:50,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Ask yourself, in the most general, the most fundamental, the most obvious, the most present manner,

125
00:08:52,224 --> 00:08:53,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I want this to disappear?

126
00:08:56,046 --> 00:08:57,586
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where that aversion is rooted.

127
00:08:57,967 --> 00:09:00,788
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like a subtle moment that you must catch.

128
00:09:01,488 --> 00:09:04,449
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like the most fundamental basis of your mind, basically.

129
00:09:04,849 --> 00:09:07,491
[SPEAKER_01]: The ultimate peripheral in regard to the arisen experience.

130
00:09:07,551 --> 00:09:08,551
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where craving is.

131
00:09:09,031 --> 00:09:10,312
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where you can't see it directly.

132
00:09:11,290 --> 00:09:22,713
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're too concerned with the particulars in terms of, well, there's fear and then there is this and there is this, but failing to see that all of that is already within the attitude for the sake of getting rid of fear.

133
00:09:24,934 --> 00:09:26,134
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what you want to change.

134
00:09:26,514 --> 00:09:32,796
[SPEAKER_01]: You change that, then you don't care about anything particular that arises on account of fear, because you're not trying to get rid of it.

135
00:09:34,817 --> 00:09:35,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

136
00:09:35,217 --> 00:09:38,338
[SPEAKER_01]: So, instead of trying to figure it out on account of perception and the fear,

137
00:09:39,629 --> 00:09:41,211
[SPEAKER_01]: Start looking back, basically.

138
00:09:41,251 --> 00:09:49,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Start looking in the other direction, whether you still crave to get rid of the arisen feeling on account of which you're perceiving and fearing and so on.

139
00:09:50,021 --> 00:09:52,303
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's always behind you.

140
00:09:53,064 --> 00:09:54,986
[SPEAKER_01]: The feeling is always behind you, so to speak.

141
00:09:55,507 --> 00:09:57,369
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't perceive your feeling in front of you.

142
00:09:57,889 --> 00:10:01,133
[SPEAKER_01]: If you do, it's a sensation, which means it's not a feeling, it's a perception.

143
00:10:04,446 --> 00:10:05,046
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

144
00:10:05,066 --> 00:10:11,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop thinking there's this that I perceived, that then result in this fear, that now I'm averse to it, or I'm grieving, or I'm delighting.

145
00:10:12,011 --> 00:10:20,256
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that, all of that is already within the general attitude of you not wanting to feel it.

146
00:10:20,276 --> 00:10:26,260
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start discerning that general attitude, no particular can cause any issues.

147
00:10:28,405 --> 00:10:32,347
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me try to put it another way, and I don't think I expressed it properly.

148
00:10:32,908 --> 00:10:35,149
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, so, like, let's say fear, yeah?

149
00:10:35,669 --> 00:10:38,811
[SPEAKER_00]: And I have...

150
00:10:39,191 --> 00:10:43,654
[SPEAKER_00]: I noticed that the perception, or like, you know, whatever perception caused the fear, that the fear is already here.

151
00:10:44,474 --> 00:10:48,197
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like...

152
00:10:50,218 --> 00:10:56,722
[SPEAKER_00]: I see that I'm trying to get rid of it, but then I notice that, so I sort of step back from that.

153
00:10:58,523 --> 00:10:58,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

154
00:10:59,564 --> 00:11:01,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're trying to get rid of what you're trying to get rid of?

155
00:11:02,285 --> 00:11:03,746
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I'm... just let it be.

156
00:11:04,286 --> 00:11:15,611
[SPEAKER_00]: Like... fear had got nothing to do with... sort of like... it's already arisen, so I can't really go and meddle with it.

157
00:11:17,252 --> 00:11:18,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Meddle with it.

158
00:11:19,633 --> 00:11:19,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

159
00:11:20,353 --> 00:11:20,594
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

160
00:11:20,634 --> 00:11:23,315
[SPEAKER_01]: But you still had something to... you still had to say that to yourself.

161
00:11:24,335 --> 00:11:25,396
[SPEAKER_00]: No, but like...

162
00:11:25,556 --> 00:11:27,777
[SPEAKER_01]: I would... No, you just... you just said that.

163
00:11:28,686 --> 00:11:34,207
[SPEAKER_01]: You would catch yourself trying to get rid of it, then you say to yourself, or just let it be?

164
00:11:35,268 --> 00:11:35,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Not even that.

165
00:11:36,188 --> 00:11:52,712
[SPEAKER_00]: I see myself trying to get rid of it, but I would still abandon... like, while letting things be, I would abandon the... the...

166
00:11:52,752 --> 00:11:54,453
[SPEAKER_00]: I would see it as a perception of delight.

167
00:11:56,465 --> 00:11:59,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you don't really perceive your delight.

168
00:12:02,591 --> 00:12:03,952
[SPEAKER_01]: You delight in what you perceive.

169
00:12:04,052 --> 00:12:04,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

170
00:12:05,573 --> 00:12:06,434
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, yeah.

171
00:12:06,474 --> 00:12:07,155
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

172
00:12:07,275 --> 00:12:16,243
[SPEAKER_01]: You're sort of, like, it's necessary, it's inevitable way, but you are overly analyzing it as these components that you're leaving, that you're trying to abandon.

173
00:12:16,984 --> 00:12:18,645
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that is rooted in your...

174
00:12:18,725 --> 00:12:21,288
[SPEAKER_01]: I must do something in regard to this.

175
00:12:21,879 --> 00:12:27,964
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's rooted in basically, I will either indulge this, or I will get rid of this, depending on how you feel.

176
00:12:29,485 --> 00:12:34,649
[SPEAKER_01]: The fundamental aspect which is craving, in regard to what's being felt.

177
00:12:36,851 --> 00:12:39,733
[SPEAKER_00]: But when I abandon the delay,

178
00:12:41,086 --> 00:12:42,286
[SPEAKER_00]: The feeling persists.

179
00:12:42,847 --> 00:12:48,888
[SPEAKER_00]: The feeling persists as in, like, if it's unpleasant, it persists as unpleasant, as long as it persists.

180
00:12:49,669 --> 00:12:54,870
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, I'm not doing anything with the feeling.

181
00:12:56,151 --> 00:13:01,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Well... How can you delight without feeling a delight?

182
00:13:02,172 --> 00:13:03,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Without feeling a prospect of pleasure?

183
00:13:06,274 --> 00:13:06,874
[SPEAKER_01]: What else is it?

184
00:13:06,934 --> 00:13:08,914
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the delight then, if it's not that?

185
00:13:09,595 --> 00:13:10,455
[SPEAKER_01]: Prospect of pleasure.

186
00:13:20,159 --> 00:13:20,679
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

187
00:13:20,699 --> 00:13:27,184
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you place the delight in the perception where you think you can see it, and then you're like, okay, and now this is the feeling.

188
00:13:27,784 --> 00:13:29,765
[SPEAKER_01]: None of those things are in front of you in that manner.

189
00:13:32,207 --> 00:13:32,367
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

190
00:13:32,647 --> 00:13:40,973
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, through doing it like that, I'm pointing out that you should start seeing that they're already there, including, including,

191
00:13:41,687 --> 00:13:45,528
[SPEAKER_01]: That delight, and you're either confirming or trying to get your delight.

192
00:13:46,009 --> 00:13:58,133
[SPEAKER_01]: So, instead of going through with the action of doing it, you actually include that in that mindfulness properly established peripherally as of something that has arisen beforehand.

193
00:13:58,233 --> 00:13:59,033
[SPEAKER_01]: It's already there.

194
00:13:59,574 --> 00:14:04,095
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any effort you make, it's as a result of that which has already arisen there, present for you.

195
00:14:05,076 --> 00:14:06,796
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start including your actions.

196
00:14:06,896 --> 00:14:08,037
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you surmount them.

197
00:14:08,117 --> 00:14:11,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by following through with them, so to speak.

198
00:14:13,302 --> 00:14:14,822
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how the mind truly goes beyond.

199
00:14:15,082 --> 00:14:19,523
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, inevitably, you have to follow through with this action until the nature becomes apparent.

200
00:14:20,023 --> 00:14:22,624
[SPEAKER_01]: But in order to surmount the nature, you disown it.

201
00:14:22,784 --> 00:14:23,684
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you surmount it.

202
00:14:24,104 --> 00:14:30,546
[SPEAKER_01]: And you disown it when you include it into the arisen experience, not when you follow through with it and get a desirable result.

203
00:14:32,306 --> 00:14:33,926
[SPEAKER_01]: There is still sense of self there involved.

204
00:14:37,187 --> 00:14:41,628
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, there is something you heard outside, the fear has arisen,

205
00:14:43,312 --> 00:14:45,853
[SPEAKER_01]: There is... you start discerning what else is there.

206
00:14:46,073 --> 00:14:46,654
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't stop.

207
00:14:46,934 --> 00:14:47,274
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

208
00:14:47,294 --> 00:14:48,195
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is the fear.

209
00:14:48,455 --> 00:14:49,535
[SPEAKER_01]: I must find my delight.

210
00:14:49,575 --> 00:14:50,836
[SPEAKER_01]: You're already doing something.

211
00:14:51,656 --> 00:14:53,577
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start discerning what's already there.

212
00:14:53,897 --> 00:14:55,358
[SPEAKER_01]: So, something you heard.

213
00:14:55,718 --> 00:14:56,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Fear is arisen.

214
00:14:57,632 --> 00:15:11,903
[SPEAKER_01]: There is the attitude of unpleasant, which means there is the attitude, I don't want it there, which means there is a degree of craving, of trying to get rid of the original experience, which then means that when you start looking for delight, you're doing it for that reason.

215
00:15:12,263 --> 00:15:25,633
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you can't stop looking for where the delight is, where the problem is, but you include that as well, as that which has arisen on account of the sound, on account of the feeling, on account of the fear, on account of the craving, on account of the delight.

216
00:15:26,413 --> 00:15:34,936
[SPEAKER_01]: If none of those things were there, simultaneously still enduring, your efforts of trying to see through with that delight and remove it, would not be there either.

217
00:15:35,336 --> 00:15:36,716
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you actually uproot it.

218
00:15:41,598 --> 00:15:45,759
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why when in the Sutta, Swami's Buddha kind of seemingly does the analysis of things.

219
00:15:45,779 --> 00:15:50,761
[SPEAKER_01]: It's merely just describing what's there, so that you can basically include it all, and that's how you disown it.

220
00:15:51,381 --> 00:15:54,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by going 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and then the goal is to arrive at the 10.

221
00:15:58,510 --> 00:16:11,379
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, like, that's the thing again, like, um... the abandonment is never really resulting in anything but sort of ease, like, for the moment.

222
00:16:11,699 --> 00:16:12,940
[SPEAKER_01]: So, well, that's quite a result.

223
00:16:13,260 --> 00:16:16,923
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can you say abandonment is not resulting in anything except ease?

224
00:16:18,064 --> 00:16:21,106
[SPEAKER_00]: For the moment... in the sense, like, I have to go through that again the next time.

225
00:16:22,969 --> 00:16:27,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, so, whatever is arisen, there's a lust.

226
00:16:28,252 --> 00:16:32,035
[SPEAKER_00]: The lustful thought is there, arisen.

227
00:16:32,555 --> 00:16:38,419
[SPEAKER_00]: And just by abandoning it, the pull is still there.

228
00:16:38,939 --> 00:16:42,261
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you abandon it?

229
00:16:47,024 --> 00:16:48,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Not by continuing to delight in it.

230
00:16:49,540 --> 00:16:51,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you delight in it in the first place, then?

231
00:16:51,420 --> 00:16:52,261
[SPEAKER_00]: Delight is a reason.

232
00:16:52,941 --> 00:16:54,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but why do you delight in it?

233
00:16:58,842 --> 00:17:01,663
[SPEAKER_00]: It was already there, like... Yeah, the delight.

234
00:17:01,703 --> 00:17:02,823
[SPEAKER_01]: But why is that your problem?

235
00:17:03,343 --> 00:17:04,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you have to abandon it?

236
00:17:05,004 --> 00:17:05,644
[SPEAKER_01]: That's my whole point.

237
00:17:05,664 --> 00:17:08,225
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I'm trying to talk about last 15-20 minutes.

238
00:17:08,505 --> 00:17:10,945
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's... Why do you have to do it?

239
00:17:14,826 --> 00:17:15,326
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

240
00:17:15,346 --> 00:17:17,787
[SPEAKER_01]: You keep perceiving, I must do something.

241
00:17:18,673 --> 00:17:21,214
[SPEAKER_01]: You keep saying no, but I see it's exactly what I'm talking about.

242
00:17:22,575 --> 00:17:23,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it impresses you.

243
00:17:24,116 --> 00:17:24,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

244
00:17:24,777 --> 00:17:25,457
[SPEAKER_01]: It's unpleasant.

245
00:17:25,757 --> 00:17:28,539
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you must do something about it to get rid of the displeasure.

246
00:17:29,839 --> 00:17:35,683
[SPEAKER_01]: We arrived at how I started it.

247
00:17:35,763 --> 00:17:39,225
[SPEAKER_01]: The delight in it affects you because it's unpleasant.

248
00:17:40,343 --> 00:17:42,444
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to get rid of it.

249
00:17:42,544 --> 00:17:43,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it hurts.

250
00:17:43,825 --> 00:17:44,226
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

251
00:17:44,446 --> 00:17:45,586
[SPEAKER_00]: The hurt actually...

252
00:17:45,606 --> 00:17:48,288
[SPEAKER_01]: It will never see you mixing the two again.

253
00:17:49,249 --> 00:17:51,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's get back how you started describing it.

254
00:17:51,190 --> 00:17:51,950
[SPEAKER_01]: You said it yourself.

255
00:17:52,771 --> 00:17:53,612
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let's stick with that.

256
00:17:54,472 --> 00:17:55,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality arises.

257
00:17:55,773 --> 00:17:55,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

258
00:17:56,153 --> 00:17:56,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Delight in it.

259
00:17:57,114 --> 00:17:58,034
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you abandon it.

260
00:17:58,154 --> 00:17:58,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you abandon it?

261
00:18:00,290 --> 00:18:01,471
[SPEAKER_01]: You say, because the delight has arisen.

262
00:18:01,551 --> 00:18:02,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that your problem?

263
00:18:02,992 --> 00:18:05,914
[SPEAKER_01]: If the delight has arisen, why do you have to abandon it?

264
00:18:06,594 --> 00:18:08,355
[SPEAKER_00]: I abandon continuation.

265
00:18:08,475 --> 00:18:08,716
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

266
00:18:09,036 --> 00:18:13,299
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not asking you what you do, I'm asking you why you're doing it.

267
00:18:16,120 --> 00:18:28,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I'm... because it's unwholesome to... or rather, because... depend... like, not seeing that I've been...

268
00:18:31,282 --> 00:18:33,184
[SPEAKER_00]: I've been pressed by the feeling.

269
00:18:33,844 --> 00:18:41,491
[SPEAKER_00]: I continue to indulge in it, or rather, I proliferate on that, like, lust.

270
00:18:42,152 --> 00:18:44,394
[SPEAKER_00]: I continue to, sort of, chase after it.

271
00:18:44,454 --> 00:18:52,501
[SPEAKER_00]: But the moment I step back, I see that I... it hurts, that hurt...

272
00:18:52,662 --> 00:18:54,964
[SPEAKER_01]: I say that... Yeah, well, see, all of that is secondary.

273
00:18:56,035 --> 00:19:01,119
[SPEAKER_01]: I ask you, why do you need to abandon the delight in the first place?

274
00:19:01,539 --> 00:19:05,342
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you're telling me... Now you're telling me all the reasons that happens after and so on.

275
00:19:05,382 --> 00:19:06,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because it hurts.

276
00:19:06,583 --> 00:19:08,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it hurts, exactly.

277
00:19:08,784 --> 00:19:12,647
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the point of converging, basically, and that's what I keep trying to point at.

278
00:19:13,737 --> 00:19:14,517
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inevitable.

279
00:19:14,537 --> 00:19:16,198
[SPEAKER_01]: You're going to be trying to manage it.

280
00:19:16,298 --> 00:19:17,459
[SPEAKER_01]: You're going to be trying to do it.

281
00:19:17,839 --> 00:19:20,740
[SPEAKER_01]: But you'll never succeed properly until you start seeing this point.

282
00:19:20,860 --> 00:19:24,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is that other direction that I'm talking about.

283
00:19:24,542 --> 00:19:26,723
[SPEAKER_01]: And that direction is whereby it hurts.

284
00:19:27,183 --> 00:19:28,504
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the sole problem.

285
00:19:29,144 --> 00:19:31,805
[SPEAKER_01]: If it doesn't hurt, you would stop caring about it.

286
00:19:32,265 --> 00:19:35,447
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't stop caring about it because you take hurt for granted.

287
00:19:35,747 --> 00:19:42,930
[SPEAKER_01]: And you take hurt for granted through your acts of abandoning or doing this or doing that because, you know, it's unwholesome if I don't do it and so on.

288
00:19:44,581 --> 00:19:47,061
[SPEAKER_01]: All that is true, but none of that is the reason why.

289
00:19:47,081 --> 00:19:48,862
[SPEAKER_01]: The reason why is because you don't want to hurt.

290
00:19:49,782 --> 00:19:50,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

291
00:19:50,862 --> 00:19:55,663
[SPEAKER_01]: But you hurt because you appropriate the disagreeable, not because you hurt.

292
00:19:57,544 --> 00:20:00,484
[SPEAKER_01]: And you already hinted at that when you said the delight has arisen.

293
00:20:00,984 --> 00:20:01,544
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

294
00:20:01,744 --> 00:20:06,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Include that in the arisen thing, not in you.

295
00:20:06,325 --> 00:20:06,745
[SPEAKER_01]: Say that again.

296
00:20:06,985 --> 00:20:10,186
[SPEAKER_01]: Include the disagreeable into the arisen thing.

297
00:20:12,138 --> 00:20:15,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Not as the result of the arisen thing that now affecting you.

298
00:20:18,060 --> 00:20:20,740
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I meant in that talk, nobody wants sensuality.

299
00:20:21,021 --> 00:20:23,521
[SPEAKER_01]: The pain of sensuality is sensuality.

300
00:20:23,701 --> 00:20:24,562
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not in you.

301
00:20:24,702 --> 00:20:25,982
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not because you said no.

302
00:20:26,042 --> 00:20:27,542
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not because you didn't do enough.

303
00:20:28,203 --> 00:20:30,163
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because it's unpleasant in itself.

304
00:20:31,804 --> 00:20:37,485
[SPEAKER_01]: And that can only become apparent through restraint, of course, because each time you engage with sensuality, you obscure that principle.

305
00:20:38,625 --> 00:20:40,166
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what I'm talking about here.

306
00:20:41,177 --> 00:20:47,841
[SPEAKER_01]: That pain, that sting of sensuality, then you feel like there is a delight in it.

307
00:20:47,941 --> 00:20:48,781
[SPEAKER_01]: I must stop this.

308
00:20:48,941 --> 00:20:52,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you must stop that through restraint, but that's not like seeing the nature of it.

309
00:20:53,083 --> 00:20:59,087
[SPEAKER_01]: That's management, which is necessary, but don't confuse it for uprooting of it, because it will never uproot it by itself.

310
00:20:59,487 --> 00:20:59,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

311
00:21:00,247 --> 00:21:09,212
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the point is, when you are touched by that unpleasantness, by that delight, when that fear arises or whatever else, you don't have to do anything.

312
00:21:12,952 --> 00:21:21,413
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will be able to not do anything, if you see that that delight, that touch, belongs to the arisen thing, not to you.

313
00:21:22,834 --> 00:21:25,954
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you are touched only because you make yourself so.

314
00:21:27,094 --> 00:21:31,615
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you're responsible for passa, for contact.

315
00:21:33,676 --> 00:21:40,877
[SPEAKER_01]: But as long as I'm a pūtujana, that arises... Yeah, but as long as you keep chasing your methods of management, you're gonna stay a pūtujana as well.

316
00:21:41,497 --> 00:21:41,777
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

317
00:21:42,970 --> 00:21:45,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody... nobody says you should not be restrained.

318
00:21:45,933 --> 00:21:49,576
[SPEAKER_01]: You should not keep and, you know, be mindful of your eyes, nose and so on.

319
00:21:49,636 --> 00:21:56,483
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you want to uproot it and not just rely on saying no, you actually have to start understanding the nature.

320
00:21:56,903 --> 00:21:59,045
[SPEAKER_01]: And the nature is that it has nothing to do with you.

321
00:21:59,085 --> 00:21:59,726
[SPEAKER_01]: It's independent.

322
00:21:59,826 --> 00:22:02,268
[SPEAKER_01]: It's agreeable and disagreeable.

323
00:22:04,551 --> 00:22:06,793
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I was trying to say when you gave the example of the fear.

324
00:22:10,158 --> 00:22:16,003
[SPEAKER_01]: That grief, or whatever you want to call it, that arises, belongs to that fear, belongs to that perception, not you.

325
00:22:16,323 --> 00:22:22,468
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's already what you start including in that peripheral mindfulness of the enduring experience by itself.

326
00:22:24,090 --> 00:22:33,638
[SPEAKER_01]: And from that point of view, you realize, yeah, I arrived here through restraint, through non-centrality, but at this place, I don't even have to do restraint, nor centrality.

327
00:22:35,198 --> 00:22:40,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Because both things require a form of effort, and now I'm including this in a completely impersonal manner.

328
00:22:40,961 --> 00:22:41,582
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not mine.

329
00:22:41,662 --> 00:22:43,243
[SPEAKER_01]: It has a reason to come to these things.

330
00:22:43,343 --> 00:22:44,523
[SPEAKER_01]: It's enduring by itself.

331
00:22:45,704 --> 00:22:49,566
[SPEAKER_01]: Delight, delightful objects, pleasing sights, nothing to do with you.

332
00:22:52,728 --> 00:23:00,773
[SPEAKER_01]: But the only reason they're a problem is because you make them touch you, so to speak, on the level of feeling, and then you must deal with it, exactly.

333
00:23:02,637 --> 00:23:05,580
[SPEAKER_01]: But dealing with it is management after the touch.

334
00:23:06,520 --> 00:23:07,681
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to start uprooting it.

335
00:23:07,701 --> 00:23:10,744
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to see that it's actually it's impossible to touch in the first place.

336
00:23:27,098 --> 00:23:28,439
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm not saying you should stop.

337
00:23:29,288 --> 00:23:36,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Your management and your sort of, you know, mental reflection of abandonment and stuff, but this is what you should aim at.

338
00:23:36,594 --> 00:23:41,538
[SPEAKER_01]: This is what those doings, so to speak, should be aiming at.

339
00:23:42,659 --> 00:23:47,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Not at a kind of, okay, now it has succeeded in my reflection because this thing has disappeared.

340
00:23:47,282 --> 00:23:47,423
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

341
00:23:47,443 --> 00:23:48,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Things disappear on its own.

342
00:23:49,164 --> 00:23:54,808
[SPEAKER_01]: You want them to bring you back whereby you won't have to do even that because you're not touched in the first place.

343
00:23:57,170 --> 00:23:57,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Beautiful sight.

344
00:24:00,049 --> 00:24:01,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Pain has arisen.

345
00:24:02,351 --> 00:24:05,174
[SPEAKER_01]: It's part of the beautiful experience of the beautiful sight.

346
00:24:05,354 --> 00:24:09,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Not part of you, not part of what you are doing, or what you haven't been doing.

347
00:24:10,980 --> 00:24:12,341
[SPEAKER_01]: It belongs to the sight.

348
00:24:14,243 --> 00:24:16,205
[SPEAKER_00]: It belongs to the sound, smell, taste, touch.

349
00:24:27,082 --> 00:24:29,444
[SPEAKER_01]: The simile of the poisonous drink in the suttas.

350
00:24:30,545 --> 00:24:31,946
[SPEAKER_01]: You can extend it, think about it.

351
00:24:32,607 --> 00:24:36,570
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a beautiful beverage, inside smell, consistency, everything is beautiful.

352
00:24:37,391 --> 00:24:39,753
[SPEAKER_01]: But you know it has a poison that will kill you.

353
00:24:43,456 --> 00:24:48,080
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could reflect and you could see that, yeah, the death is included in this cup, basically.

354
00:24:48,360 --> 00:24:51,603
[SPEAKER_01]: The implication of death belongs to this poisonous beverage.

355
00:24:53,204 --> 00:24:55,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, you have to go through it to do it, but

356
00:24:57,072 --> 00:24:59,934
[SPEAKER_01]: If you weren't there, no amount of doing would result in death.

357
00:25:00,775 --> 00:25:01,436
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it is there.

358
00:25:02,536 --> 00:25:08,441
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you realize the pain that you get through restraint of sensuality or whatever else, it's the sensuality.

359
00:25:09,022 --> 00:25:10,883
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your restraint is not painful.

360
00:25:11,003 --> 00:25:17,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Your restraint reveals the pain of what sensuality actually is.

361
00:25:20,931 --> 00:25:22,793
[SPEAKER_01]: And from that point of view, you don't need to do anything.

362
00:25:23,633 --> 00:25:25,415
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is not do sensuality.

363
00:25:27,765 --> 00:25:30,646
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the abandonment is not even, say, the restraint.

364
00:25:30,986 --> 00:25:32,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly, exactly.

365
00:25:33,047 --> 00:25:37,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Restraint is necessary for you to start, sort of, building up the basis for abandonment.

366
00:25:37,548 --> 00:25:43,490
[SPEAKER_01]: But abandonment is understanding, not what you need to do on account of that which has arisen and affected you.

367
00:25:44,570 --> 00:25:47,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you understood it, you won't... That's why the contact ceases.

368
00:25:49,772 --> 00:25:53,453
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha would say, for one who has the right view and who practices correctly.

369
00:25:54,033 --> 00:25:54,873
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not touched.

370
00:25:55,013 --> 00:25:57,194
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the definition of cessation of the contact.

371
00:25:57,741 --> 00:26:07,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind you, when I speak about a contact, you have to dismiss completely the commentarial description of it, like one arm, two arms, the clasp, the sound is the contact.

372
00:26:07,871 --> 00:26:08,972
[SPEAKER_01]: It's completely impersonal.

373
00:26:08,992 --> 00:26:09,052
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

374
00:26:09,092 --> 00:26:11,074
[SPEAKER_01]: Contact means you are touched.

375
00:26:12,355 --> 00:26:15,598
[SPEAKER_01]: It means a sense of self, I am, is involved.

376
00:26:15,899 --> 00:26:21,184
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not impersonal description of how I works, how I and sight mechanism operates.

377
00:26:21,244 --> 00:26:22,185
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's not contact.

378
00:26:23,995 --> 00:26:30,801
[SPEAKER_01]: And as the Buddha said, I and forms are already out there, and consciousness is already out there.

379
00:26:31,221 --> 00:26:33,843
[SPEAKER_01]: The corresponding engagement of it is the contact.

380
00:26:34,304 --> 00:26:40,288
[SPEAKER_01]: The corresponding engagement is determined by the presence of avijja or absence of avijja.

381
00:26:41,109 --> 00:26:46,834
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, if you are ignorant, you are contacted.

382
00:26:47,603 --> 00:26:48,464
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I'm talking about.

383
00:26:48,484 --> 00:26:50,205
[SPEAKER_01]: When I say it touches you, it affects you.

384
00:26:50,245 --> 00:26:51,046
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all contact.

385
00:26:51,726 --> 00:26:58,231
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a metaphysical coming together of random things that then results in some secondary sort of experience.

386
00:26:59,091 --> 00:26:59,211
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

387
00:26:59,452 --> 00:27:00,172
[SPEAKER_01]: You are touched.

388
00:27:00,352 --> 00:27:01,593
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to do something about it.

389
00:27:03,334 --> 00:27:04,035
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not touched.

390
00:27:04,295 --> 00:27:05,816
[SPEAKER_01]: You're free from having anything to do.

391
00:27:06,236 --> 00:27:09,259
[SPEAKER_01]: You're free from any burden of duty, any burden of management.

392
00:27:10,940 --> 00:27:13,542
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for a kutujana, there's less contact when mindful.

393
00:27:14,610 --> 00:27:16,314
[SPEAKER_01]: In a way, yeah, you can say that.

394
00:27:16,374 --> 00:27:19,442
[SPEAKER_01]: He's already beginning to practice, if he practices correctly, yeah.

395
00:27:20,315 --> 00:27:32,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he'll be just fueling and reinforcing the contact through dependence on his wrong view of management, on his meditation techniques, psychotherapy, whatever he might be doing on account of the suffering that has arisen.

396
00:27:33,397 --> 00:27:36,018
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I kept going back to it when I say, how does he feel?

397
00:27:36,358 --> 00:27:36,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Unpleasant.

398
00:27:37,178 --> 00:27:39,118
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the sole reason for your action.

399
00:27:39,139 --> 00:27:41,439
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why craving is the root of suffering.

400
00:27:41,479 --> 00:27:42,639
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

401
00:27:42,659 --> 00:27:47,020
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove that, there is nothing for you to do, because you don't suffer in the first place.

402
00:27:49,892 --> 00:27:52,334
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and again, as we said, sometimes it can be like this.

403
00:27:52,354 --> 00:27:59,641
[SPEAKER_01]: So, oh, if it's a delight, or if it's this, or if it's that, all of that, is it on account of that, a reason contact that touched you, basically.

404
00:28:00,542 --> 00:28:00,983
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that.

405
00:28:01,383 --> 00:28:11,533
[SPEAKER_01]: So, instead of you chasing those sort of loose ends, trying to manage them, you might do it sometimes, if you don't have enough mindfulness, which is basically on a form of restraint.

406
00:28:13,120 --> 00:28:15,342
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't want to confuse that with a practice.

407
00:28:15,482 --> 00:28:15,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

408
00:28:15,762 --> 00:28:21,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you do, then you won't be looking for what the actual practice is that will result in uprooting it in the first place.

409
00:28:22,288 --> 00:28:28,233
[SPEAKER_01]: And you start uprooting it when you start including it into the arisen experience that is enduring by itself.

410
00:28:29,438 --> 00:28:32,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Changing while standing or persisting while changing, depending on translation.

411
00:28:33,442 --> 00:28:34,783
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you want to start discerning.

412
00:28:34,823 --> 00:28:43,550
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you realize, oh, even these acts that are now occurring, that are now becoming possible, are within this endurance of this arisen experience as a whole.

413
00:28:44,731 --> 00:28:45,452
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in you.

414
00:28:45,652 --> 00:28:46,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Not on you.

415
00:28:48,294 --> 00:28:51,017
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how prospect of delight doesn't make you delight.

416
00:28:52,422 --> 00:28:55,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Prospect of getting rid of it, doesn't make you try and get rid of it.

417
00:28:55,404 --> 00:28:57,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Prospect of indulging it, doesn't make you indulge it.

418
00:28:58,326 --> 00:29:00,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you... it belongs to the thing, not to you.

419
00:29:00,367 --> 00:29:01,468
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not your prospect.

420
00:29:01,928 --> 00:29:03,109
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not your possibility.

421
00:29:04,870 --> 00:29:06,591
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not your option.

422
00:29:06,631 --> 00:29:11,314
[SPEAKER_01]: So, abandonment is... it's not something you do it... Abandonment is full understanding.

423
00:29:11,334 --> 00:29:12,395
[SPEAKER_01]: Abandonment is uprooting.

424
00:29:13,116 --> 00:29:13,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Cessation, basically.

425
00:29:13,976 --> 00:29:14,957
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what abandonment is.

426
00:29:17,211 --> 00:29:21,174
[SPEAKER_01]: But as I said, yeah, don't now think, okay, so I should not practice restraint.

427
00:29:21,294 --> 00:29:22,234
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's one thing.

428
00:29:23,315 --> 00:29:25,917
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm saying don't confuse restraint with the second thing.

429
00:29:26,297 --> 00:29:28,038
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the practice of dhamma.

430
00:29:28,579 --> 00:29:31,481
[SPEAKER_01]: The abandonment, the uprooting, the extinguishing and so on.

431
00:29:32,881 --> 00:29:34,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is, you know, happens just too easily.

432
00:29:46,199 --> 00:29:54,307
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you feel like it's necessary to go through a certain mental management of whatever touched you, that's fine, but regard it as a form of restraint.

433
00:29:55,128 --> 00:30:00,954
[SPEAKER_01]: Regard it as a form of leaving the crowd, or not looking at enticing sight, and so on.

434
00:30:02,215 --> 00:30:09,063
[SPEAKER_01]: And once you kind of fulfill, okay, now it's sort of restrained enough, then you start looking including that into the original thing.

435
00:30:09,975 --> 00:30:10,896
[SPEAKER_01]: Start uprooting it.

436
00:30:11,016 --> 00:30:12,077
[SPEAKER_01]: Start surmounting it.

437
00:30:12,537 --> 00:30:14,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't think that's where your work ends.

438
00:30:14,138 --> 00:30:18,541
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where your work begins, after you're restrained.

439
00:30:19,162 --> 00:30:20,903
[SPEAKER_01]: But that work is the work you can't do.

440
00:30:22,544 --> 00:30:30,470
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't be performing certain actions that will result in... Well, you can only indirectly perform certain actions that result in freedom from action, in uprooting the action.

441
00:30:31,992 --> 00:30:38,837
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the including, the mindfulness I'm talking about, that's done on the basis of restraint, is not something you can do as a method.

442
00:30:39,567 --> 00:30:41,028
[SPEAKER_01]: It's either you see it or you don't.

443
00:30:41,188 --> 00:30:42,868
[SPEAKER_01]: If not, you have to make the effort to see it.

444
00:30:43,008 --> 00:30:44,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you see it, it's effortless.

445
00:30:44,969 --> 00:30:46,449
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the things have a reason on their own.

446
00:30:46,829 --> 00:30:50,291
[SPEAKER_01]: And because they have a reason on their own, all you need to do is recognize that.

447
00:30:51,491 --> 00:30:55,012
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the Buddha himself just recognized the universal path of the dhamma.

448
00:30:55,032 --> 00:30:56,113
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't create any of that.

449
00:30:57,273 --> 00:30:58,113
[SPEAKER_00]: It was already there.

450
00:31:01,274 --> 00:31:05,876
[SPEAKER_01]: The pleasant abiding here and now is the pleasant abiding in the jhāna, which is the abiding in non-activity.

451
00:31:07,152 --> 00:31:07,952
[SPEAKER_00]: How it's translated.

452
00:31:08,613 --> 00:31:09,893
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how we arrived at this whole thing.

453
00:31:21,558 --> 00:31:24,139
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you can't crave for jhānas either, because you're not doing them.

454
00:31:31,262 --> 00:31:31,842
[SPEAKER_00]: You find them.

455
00:31:33,062 --> 00:31:33,983
[SPEAKER_00]: You discern them, sure.

456
00:31:34,103 --> 00:31:34,443
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

457
00:31:36,011 --> 00:31:45,534
[SPEAKER_01]: First being restrained, being... having abandoned delight, grief, aversion, whatever, in regard to the sights, sights, smells, tastes, touches, in regard to the world.

458
00:31:47,935 --> 00:31:54,897
[SPEAKER_01]: So, having been restrained, having abandoned, then you... the mind settles in, and then you start discerning it, and then you're not doing it.

459
00:31:57,098 --> 00:32:01,943
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize even when you thought or think you're doing it, you're not.

460
00:32:02,423 --> 00:32:05,366
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way you can think you're doing it is because it was given to you as an option.

461
00:32:05,827 --> 00:32:08,450
[SPEAKER_01]: And that option has arisen on its own beforehand and it's enduring.

462
00:32:09,110 --> 00:32:11,373
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you yourself is not your own.

463
00:32:12,974 --> 00:32:18,400
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the difference between pūgala, individual, and atta, and sort of the assumption of self.

464
00:32:20,363 --> 00:32:21,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Intentionality stays.

465
00:32:21,585 --> 00:32:29,199
[SPEAKER_01]: You can still choose based on the given choices, but none of them will be implying the sense of self and me, or be rooted in in the wrong view of kutujana.

466
00:32:51,884 --> 00:33:00,528
[SPEAKER_01]: So, practically speaking, my jewel is pretty practical, but let's give a practical example.

467
00:33:00,568 --> 00:33:04,050
[SPEAKER_01]: An inciting thought, a prospect has arisen.

468
00:33:05,070 --> 00:33:07,571
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there is a pleasing sight outside.

469
00:33:09,292 --> 00:33:10,993
[SPEAKER_01]: And you feel the pull to go and see it.

470
00:33:13,334 --> 00:33:14,895
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first is physical restraint.

471
00:33:15,175 --> 00:33:15,855
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't do it.

472
00:33:16,796 --> 00:33:18,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, mind still feels touched by it.

473
00:33:19,257 --> 00:33:20,898
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're not trying to get rid of the touch.

474
00:33:21,882 --> 00:33:31,469
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not trying to get rid of all the prospects, the, oh, because the thoughts, oh, but if I could, you know, if I just look, or if I open the door, or if I go, or if I do this, if I do that.

475
00:33:31,789 --> 00:33:33,110
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not trying to rid of any of that.

476
00:33:33,150 --> 00:33:34,011
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not trying to manage it.

477
00:33:34,371 --> 00:33:37,433
[SPEAKER_00]: All you want to do is stop being touched by that whole thing.

478
00:33:38,914 --> 00:33:40,175
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you uproot sensuality.

479
00:33:40,215 --> 00:33:47,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by trying to prevent these sensual possibilities from arising.

480
00:33:49,081 --> 00:33:50,782
[SPEAKER_01]: They are only a problem because you are touched.

481
00:33:50,842 --> 00:33:55,525
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to remove the touch, then they just stay there, hollow, dead, lifeless.

482
00:33:56,406 --> 00:33:57,626
[SPEAKER_01]: They are not your possibility.

483
00:33:58,287 --> 00:33:59,828
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no delight in them then.

484
00:34:00,308 --> 00:34:01,509
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no desire for them.

485
00:34:03,250 --> 00:34:04,250
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you remove touch?

486
00:34:04,811 --> 00:34:05,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry?

487
00:34:05,291 --> 00:34:06,212
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you remove touch?

488
00:34:06,652 --> 00:34:08,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I've been talking about it for last 40 minutes.

489
00:34:10,667 --> 00:34:11,848
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't... how do you remove touch?

490
00:34:11,868 --> 00:34:13,569
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you do this, then you do this, then you do this.

491
00:34:13,649 --> 00:34:13,809
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

492
00:34:13,929 --> 00:34:16,451
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that would be always done on account of being touched.

493
00:34:17,032 --> 00:34:20,054
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you remove touch by discerning how you're being affected.

494
00:34:20,874 --> 00:34:25,077
[SPEAKER_01]: The discerning that that's the problem and then seeing that that problem doesn't belong to you.

495
00:34:26,539 --> 00:34:28,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you people can say, oh, this is not my problem, this is not my problem.

496
00:34:28,900 --> 00:34:39,268
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you do that attitude of this is not my problem, as in doesn't belong to me, too soon, means there are things you haven't seen that are still made yours and thus your problem.

497
00:34:40,134 --> 00:34:43,776
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to come down to the point of convergence that I'm talking about.

498
00:34:44,856 --> 00:34:48,778
[SPEAKER_01]: The point of touch from which you end up doing all these acts.

499
00:34:49,738 --> 00:34:53,900
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, see that point of contact as not belonging to you.

500
00:34:54,960 --> 00:34:56,801
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, it's not a momentary thing.

501
00:34:56,841 --> 00:34:57,481
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like contact.

502
00:34:57,521 --> 00:34:58,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I missed it.

503
00:34:59,222 --> 00:35:01,683
[SPEAKER_01]: For as long as there is feeling, there is a touch there.

504
00:35:02,143 --> 00:35:03,364
[SPEAKER_01]: You are being contacted.

505
00:35:04,940 --> 00:35:08,322
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you have immediate verification when you surmount the contact.

506
00:35:08,782 --> 00:35:12,184
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't touch you, yet it's still there.

507
00:35:12,384 --> 00:35:18,188
[SPEAKER_01]: The inciting prospect is still there, the object you wanted to look in the first place is still there, but the pressure is gone.

508
00:35:19,568 --> 00:35:23,771
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because you've done some perfect management, it's because you surmounted it.

509
00:35:23,791 --> 00:35:26,212
[SPEAKER_01]: You saw it as not belonging to you at the right level.

510
00:35:27,313 --> 00:35:32,736
[SPEAKER_01]: The level being the level of feeling, the level of trying to crave in regard to getting rid of the unpleasant feeling.

511
00:35:33,898 --> 00:35:38,700
[SPEAKER_01]: You will stop trying to get rid of the unpleasant feeling, when you see that unpleasant feeling is not your problem.

512
00:35:39,260 --> 00:35:40,740
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not your displeasure.

513
00:35:41,501 --> 00:35:43,982
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the displeasure of that thing that's in front of you, that's pulling you.

514
00:35:48,123 --> 00:35:49,264
[SPEAKER_01]: That pleasing thing.

515
00:35:52,305 --> 00:36:01,328
[SPEAKER_00]: But you only surmount contact when... Like, basically, surmounting contact means you're a... you're a Arya.

516
00:36:03,105 --> 00:36:07,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, how do you think Puthujan can become an Ārya, unless he keeps trying to understand what Ārya is?

517
00:36:10,910 --> 00:36:12,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, he can mean that, say he means that.

518
00:36:12,912 --> 00:36:13,132
[SPEAKER_01]: So?

519
00:36:13,532 --> 00:36:14,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there a reason to not try to do it?

520
00:36:16,194 --> 00:36:16,774
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't understand.

521
00:36:19,296 --> 00:36:20,497
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you think Puthujan surmounts?

522
00:36:20,517 --> 00:36:22,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you think he surmounts contact before and then he becomes an Ārya?

523
00:36:24,140 --> 00:36:27,202
[SPEAKER_01]: Or he does something else and then magically he learns how to surmount the contact?

524
00:36:27,382 --> 00:36:27,522
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

525
00:36:28,735 --> 00:36:31,196
[SPEAKER_01]: Puthujjana becomes ārya by trying to understand what ārya is.

526
00:36:31,736 --> 00:36:33,517
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the only method you can have.

527
00:36:44,161 --> 00:36:44,701
[SPEAKER_01]: And there you go.

528
00:36:45,561 --> 00:36:47,022
[SPEAKER_01]: You just outline the work for yourself.

529
00:36:48,382 --> 00:36:55,325
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're touched by unpleasant feeling, when you're touched by the delight in pleasant feeling, which is also unpleasant because you're trying to restrain, that's it.

530
00:36:55,925 --> 00:36:57,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Deal with it and you'll become an ārya.

531
00:36:57,726 --> 00:36:58,146
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing else.

532
00:36:59,069 --> 00:37:05,895
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no mystical... mystical secret of the universe that you still haven't been sort of... that you need to reveal.

533
00:37:06,235 --> 00:37:06,375
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

534
00:37:06,596 --> 00:37:06,996
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

535
00:37:07,416 --> 00:37:08,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Surmount the touch.

536
00:37:09,578 --> 00:37:11,160
[SPEAKER_01]: Surmount the domain of sensuality.

537
00:37:11,960 --> 00:37:12,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Surmount the craving.

538
00:37:14,142 --> 00:37:14,863
[SPEAKER_01]: And you'll be on your way.

539
00:37:16,025 --> 00:37:22,467
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you carry the tools for becoming ārya-sāvaka, for becoming an arahant, right where you... in your own feelings.

540
00:37:22,587 --> 00:37:23,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Nowhere else.

541
00:37:23,147 --> 00:37:26,528
[SPEAKER_01]: No left, no right, no before, no after, not up or down from it.

542
00:37:26,628 --> 00:37:28,069
[SPEAKER_01]: It's right there, while it's present.

543
00:37:28,169 --> 00:37:28,889
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's always present.

544
00:37:28,909 --> 00:37:29,749
[SPEAKER_01]: You always feel something.

545
00:37:30,250 --> 00:37:31,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means there's always craving in regard to something.

546
00:37:31,970 --> 00:37:34,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means there's always possibility to become an ārya in regard to something.

547
00:37:35,231 --> 00:37:36,612
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to keep doing it.

548
00:37:38,312 --> 00:37:40,633
[SPEAKER_01]: And not misunderstanding it for something else that you do.

549
00:37:42,056 --> 00:37:46,900
[SPEAKER_01]: As in restraint and management to see, pertaining directly to and becoming an Arya.

550
00:37:46,940 --> 00:37:47,281
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't.

551
00:37:47,961 --> 00:37:49,843
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Sīla Bhātta Paramāsa is a fetter.

552
00:37:51,664 --> 00:37:52,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Virtue and duty.

553
00:37:53,126 --> 00:37:54,647
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what restraint is, basically.

554
00:37:54,667 --> 00:37:56,368
[SPEAKER_01]: It's my duty and it's my virtue.

555
00:37:56,929 --> 00:38:01,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, they would have said that's a fetter in regard to dhamma.

556
00:38:03,354 --> 00:38:08,599
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you surmount the fetter of virtue and duty without becoming non-virtuous and undutiful?

557
00:38:10,191 --> 00:38:14,556
[SPEAKER_01]: You surmount it by first developing it and then by uprooting it in the way I just described.

558
00:38:14,596 --> 00:38:20,623
[SPEAKER_01]: By seeing that the whole thing, management, even the good management, is actually rooted in the original thing, not in you.

559
00:38:20,703 --> 00:38:23,245
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you can... you will automatically stop doing it.

560
00:38:23,826 --> 00:38:24,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Then it's not a matter of choice.

561
00:38:25,408 --> 00:38:27,170
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't mean you become unrestrained.

562
00:38:28,970 --> 00:38:32,232
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Buddha would say, but the virtue has been developed beforehand.

563
00:38:32,913 --> 00:38:39,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, he developed the right view, he sort of freed himself from sensuality and so on, because the virtue has been developed beforehand.

564
00:38:39,657 --> 00:38:42,318
[SPEAKER_01]: He stopped doing the bad things, and then he uprooted even the good things.

565
00:38:53,446 --> 00:38:55,427
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the way to virtue is so...

566
00:38:56,107 --> 00:38:58,669
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a necessary basis, but it's not the dhamma.

567
00:38:59,823 --> 00:39:04,205
[SPEAKER_01]: If you conflate the two, you won't be looking for what dhamma is in regard to the necessary basis.

568
00:39:05,265 --> 00:39:05,386
[SPEAKER_01]: i.e.

569
00:39:05,586 --> 00:39:06,386
[SPEAKER_01]: it's gonna fetter you.

570
00:39:08,467 --> 00:39:15,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Holding on to your view of virtue and duty will fetter you from seeing what dhamma is in regard to virtue and duty.

571
00:39:17,411 --> 00:39:21,173
[SPEAKER_01]: As they said, there is no dhamma without virtue and duty.

572
00:39:23,033 --> 00:39:26,855
[SPEAKER_01]: But dhamma is not virtue and duty.

573
00:39:26,875 --> 00:39:28,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Virtue and duty comes within.

574
00:39:29,513 --> 00:39:30,834
[SPEAKER_00]: The parameters of dhamma.

575
00:39:31,674 --> 00:39:33,496
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, not necessarily.

576
00:39:34,136 --> 00:39:35,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, depends what you mean by that, basically.

577
00:39:36,998 --> 00:39:40,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Comes within parameters of dhamma, means like, ah, so my virtue and duty pertains to the dhamma.

578
00:39:41,020 --> 00:39:41,421
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't.

579
00:39:43,702 --> 00:39:45,643
[SPEAKER_01]: It pertains to the non-virtue and non-duty.

580
00:39:46,924 --> 00:39:48,445
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what virtue and duty pertains to.

581
00:39:48,545 --> 00:39:49,466
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it's bound with.

582
00:39:50,707 --> 00:39:52,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Dhamma is beyond both.

583
00:39:54,009 --> 00:39:58,592
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you understand, I'm trying, like, understanding where dhamma is, means becoming a sotāpanna.

584
00:39:59,173 --> 00:40:01,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Means freeing yourself from sīla, bhātta, paramāsa.

585
00:40:03,195 --> 00:40:04,535
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, you can ponder on that.

586
00:40:06,396 --> 00:40:08,157
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a valid line of reflection.

587
00:40:12,139 --> 00:40:12,579
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

588
00:40:12,679 --> 00:40:13,880
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't do the dhamma.

589
00:40:14,180 --> 00:40:18,422
[SPEAKER_01]: In a sense, you can do your virtue and do your duty, or do sensuality and non-duty.

590
00:40:20,162 --> 00:40:22,063
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where dhamma also frees you from karma.

591
00:40:22,744 --> 00:40:25,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Full understanding of dhamma results in cessation of action.

592
00:40:30,520 --> 00:40:32,141
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't result in the placement of action.

593
00:40:37,044 --> 00:40:37,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

594
00:40:38,585 --> 00:40:39,285
[SPEAKER_00]: Action as in?

595
00:40:39,645 --> 00:40:41,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Action as in anything that you do.

596
00:40:41,646 --> 00:40:42,687
[SPEAKER_00]: My intentional behavior.

597
00:40:42,847 --> 00:40:47,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, which is... you can just say intentional behavior, because you already start with a... with a view of my.

598
00:40:48,690 --> 00:40:50,411
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to make the effort to make things mine.

599
00:40:50,431 --> 00:40:51,672
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the state of Kutujana.

600
00:40:53,353 --> 00:40:54,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance is his threshold.

601
00:40:58,123 --> 00:41:01,944
[SPEAKER_00]: So, cessation of action means... Cessation of touch.

602
00:41:03,944 --> 00:41:04,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Cessation of craving.

603
00:41:06,705 --> 00:41:10,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Not action, but intention is there.

604
00:41:10,686 --> 00:41:26,769
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, from Putujan's point of view, cessation of intention, cessation of action, cessation of feeling, cessation of everything, cessation of consciousness, as the Buddha would say, means cessation of my, my action, my consciousness, my feeling.

605
00:41:29,370 --> 00:41:30,310
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

606
00:41:30,330 --> 00:41:46,456
[SPEAKER_01]: If Puthujjana understands, how can Arahant still be conscious, still have intentions, still decide things, still speak, still act, but not do so?

607
00:41:46,476 --> 00:41:48,917
[SPEAKER_01]: He will then be at least a Sotāpanna, that Puthujjana.

608
00:41:48,937 --> 00:41:53,099
[SPEAKER_01]: If he can understand, how can you have intention without my intention?

609
00:41:54,199 --> 00:41:56,660
[SPEAKER_01]: How can there be consciousness without my consciousness?

610
00:41:57,531 --> 00:42:05,361
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what we've described, like, putujana, seka and arahant themselves, the five aggregates are there.

611
00:42:06,722 --> 00:42:12,689
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no difference in the nature of form, feeling, perception, intention, sankaras and consciousness.

612
00:42:12,710 --> 00:42:13,490
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no difference.

613
00:42:14,171 --> 00:42:16,214
[SPEAKER_01]: The difference is the attitude in regard to it.

614
00:42:17,870 --> 00:42:20,412
[SPEAKER_01]: One is fully immersed in in appropriation of it.

615
00:42:20,952 --> 00:42:28,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Another one has approached the appropriation, needs to spend time basically dwelling on it, or just maintaining it, so to speak, not getting distracted from it.

616
00:42:29,218 --> 00:42:33,801
[SPEAKER_01]: And any residues of appropriation that are still left on the particular level will disappear.

617
00:42:34,282 --> 00:42:36,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Arahant is done, top to bottom.

618
00:42:37,164 --> 00:42:38,364
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the same five aggregates.

619
00:42:38,424 --> 00:42:44,409
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same intentionality, same perception, same... That's why puthujjanas can't tell the arahant, or even ārya, externally.

620
00:42:44,549 --> 00:42:46,070
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not flying in the air and having

621
00:42:46,897 --> 00:42:49,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, pink auras around him and whatnot.

622
00:42:50,758 --> 00:42:53,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Often, like, people didn't even recognize the Buddha as the Buddha.

623
00:42:53,639 --> 00:42:59,361
[SPEAKER_01]: They say, so, oh, yeah, he looks calm, but they had no idea who he was.

624
00:42:59,421 --> 00:43:01,542
[SPEAKER_01]: Or what he was.

625
00:43:01,562 --> 00:43:05,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Or the depth, depth of what he was.

626
00:43:05,423 --> 00:43:07,084
So, for, for Buddhajhā,

627
00:43:21,703 --> 00:43:22,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

628
00:43:22,383 --> 00:43:26,304
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't say five aggregates have arisen, because that implies like you know what was before.

629
00:43:26,884 --> 00:43:29,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Putujana is the five aggregates fully appropriated.

630
00:43:29,545 --> 00:43:31,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Putujana is my of the five aggregates.

631
00:43:32,806 --> 00:43:36,167
[SPEAKER_01]: The experience of Putujana is already affected with my experience.

632
00:43:36,687 --> 00:43:37,787
[SPEAKER_01]: It's already appropriated.

633
00:43:40,108 --> 00:43:43,508
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you... you don't... you can't start from first point that's clean.

634
00:43:43,848 --> 00:43:45,829
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said, a vijja is beginningless.

635
00:43:46,898 --> 00:43:47,998
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it shouldn't be there.

636
00:43:49,579 --> 00:43:54,980
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point where avijja was first manifested, before which it wasn't, does not exist.

637
00:43:55,020 --> 00:43:56,020
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha said it himself.

638
00:43:56,060 --> 00:43:57,661
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no first point of ignorance.

639
00:43:59,041 --> 00:43:59,461
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

640
00:43:59,481 --> 00:44:01,902
[SPEAKER_01]: You start with a mistake as your first point.

641
00:44:02,142 --> 00:44:03,582
[SPEAKER_01]: The mistake of appropriation.

642
00:44:04,242 --> 00:44:06,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you reverse-engineer it from there.

643
00:44:10,464 --> 00:44:15,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, now, your experience, top to bottom, is the experience of my five aggregates.

644
00:44:16,530 --> 00:44:17,170
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

645
00:44:17,450 --> 00:44:26,993
[SPEAKER_01]: You then doing the management or doing what you think you should be doing or like this or like that, is just built adding on top of that basis of already appropriated.

646
00:44:27,433 --> 00:44:29,234
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't want to be adding necessarily.

647
00:44:29,954 --> 00:44:33,255
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to do the restraint first and then just start discerning what's already there.

648
00:44:34,035 --> 00:44:40,837
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, any acts, any possibilities, any options, any management techniques that might arise, you start including them as already there.

649
00:44:41,337 --> 00:44:46,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Not... not sort of maintain the view of, I must keep doing this, because at the end of it, I'll reach the freedom from it.

650
00:44:46,478 --> 00:44:46,658
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

651
00:44:47,818 --> 00:44:51,199
[SPEAKER_01]: You reach the freedom from it by not being touched by it in the first place.

652
00:44:55,760 --> 00:44:56,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Completely the opposite direction.

653
00:44:57,501 --> 00:45:02,002
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the Ajahn Chah meant, when... like, when a person becomes Sotāpanna, feels like the whole world

654
00:45:03,078 --> 00:45:08,262
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going one way, and you are the only one who goes the other way, in every regard, pretty much.

655
00:45:10,083 --> 00:45:14,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the natural thinking, the natural... everything goes that way.

656
00:45:16,708 --> 00:45:18,589
[SPEAKER_01]: But this is completely the opposite way.

657
00:45:20,270 --> 00:45:21,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's where the roots are.

658
00:45:24,093 --> 00:45:27,796
[SPEAKER_01]: The opposite way from the branches, that keep growing and proliferating.

659
00:45:35,050 --> 00:45:38,151
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha never talks about, you know, trimming the branches.

660
00:45:38,892 --> 00:45:42,393
[SPEAKER_01]: He talks about uprooting, cutting it at its root.

661
00:45:52,037 --> 00:45:56,359
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, it's not trying to catch a specific mind moment or whatever you want to call it.

662
00:45:58,139 --> 00:45:59,200
[SPEAKER_01]: The root is there.

663
00:46:02,992 --> 00:46:05,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Throughout, it endures all the time.

664
00:46:05,434 --> 00:46:13,180
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, any experience you might have, fundamentally, the experience of the five appropriated aggregates is already there.

665
00:46:13,621 --> 00:46:15,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Throughout, it endures.

666
00:46:19,405 --> 00:46:21,207
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not trying to catch the moment.

667
00:46:21,507 --> 00:46:22,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is the route.

668
00:46:23,127 --> 00:46:35,556
[SPEAKER_01]: You're actually trying to step into it, so to speak, discern it peripherally, as already there, enduring, because of which all these things that are changing in front of you, and choosing, and this and that, are possible in the first place.

669
00:46:38,679 --> 00:46:40,620
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I meant, the point of convergence.

670
00:46:41,601 --> 00:46:45,364
[SPEAKER_01]: You're already at that level, touched, and then you do all these acts.

671
00:46:47,430 --> 00:46:50,111
[SPEAKER_01]: Failing to see that they're all done because you were touched in the first place.

672
00:46:50,431 --> 00:47:08,977
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you were to remove the possibility of being touched through this sermon of impossibility of touch in the first place, you would stop doing all of those things.

673
00:47:11,538 --> 00:47:12,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Anyway, we recorded it.

674
00:47:14,419 --> 00:47:16,400
[SPEAKER_00]: It's getting dark, so... ah, yeah.

675
00:47:18,122 --> 00:47:22,964
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, I don't want to, like, bombard you with more, because it's just the same, but you just keep releasing from the start.

676
00:47:23,725 --> 00:47:24,225
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same.

677
00:47:24,625 --> 00:47:27,386
[SPEAKER_01]: I keep talking about the same thing, obviously.

678
00:47:30,468 --> 00:47:33,810
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, see whatever you do on the level of restraint.

679
00:47:34,830 --> 00:47:41,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Do it when you feel it's necessary, but only in order to start discerning the point from which you don't have to do anything.

680
00:47:43,154 --> 00:47:47,136
[SPEAKER_01]: And from that point, engaging with sensuality or real world will become inconceivable.

681
00:47:48,140 --> 00:47:53,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that is as... as I started this, that already requires a certain dependence on doing something.

682
00:47:54,365 --> 00:47:59,510
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, on top of that dependence, well, you want to do what results in pleasure, not what results in displeasure.

683
00:48:00,231 --> 00:48:09,959
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you approach the doing in the first place, doing anything, whether pleasant or... for the sake of pleasure or unpleasure, is equally unpleasant.

684
00:48:10,720 --> 00:48:13,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you recognize doing is unpleasant in itself.

685
00:48:13,082 --> 00:48:14,363
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter how it results.

686
00:48:16,738 --> 00:48:17,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Doing is a burden.

687
00:48:18,439 --> 00:48:20,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Doing is a pressure.

688
00:48:20,699 --> 00:48:23,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Result of the doing is irrelevant, from that point of view.

689
00:48:25,301 --> 00:48:31,943
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you already develop dispassion towards doing of any kind, how can you then conceive doing sensuality?

690
00:48:33,603 --> 00:48:34,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Or doing your will?

691
00:48:34,503 --> 00:48:35,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Or doing anger?

692
00:48:35,284 --> 00:48:35,664
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

693
00:48:38,685 --> 00:48:43,006
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to penetrate, like, you have to basically... Surmount.

694
00:48:44,188 --> 00:48:46,869
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to discern it peripherally as already there.

695
00:48:48,050 --> 00:48:49,070
[SPEAKER_01]: Enduring phenomena.

696
00:48:50,211 --> 00:48:51,311
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to stop doing it.

697
00:48:52,152 --> 00:48:54,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Because what you do is what's in front of your attention.

698
00:48:54,032 --> 00:48:54,773
[SPEAKER_01]: It's what you attend.

699
00:48:56,193 --> 00:49:00,075
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't just peripherally see it just once and be done.

700
00:49:00,375 --> 00:49:03,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, because if you look peripherally, it's not peripheral anymore, isn't it?

701
00:49:04,751 --> 00:49:10,832
[SPEAKER_01]: If you look, it's what you're looking at, which means something else is peripheral in regard to your field of vision, so to speak.

702
00:49:11,432 --> 00:49:12,493
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you can't attend it.

703
00:49:12,573 --> 00:49:17,134
[SPEAKER_01]: You can just keep gradually discerning, layer by layer, of all these things that are already there.

704
00:49:19,254 --> 00:49:24,455
[SPEAKER_01]: And that attention must be confined within the virtue and duty, within sense restraint.

705
00:49:27,576 --> 00:49:28,136
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's what I mean.

706
00:49:28,156 --> 00:49:33,117
[SPEAKER_01]: In that sense, you have to protect that, so that you can discern peripheral in regard to it.

707
00:49:35,248 --> 00:49:39,429
[SPEAKER_01]: But protecting that virtue and duty is not the discerning of peripheral.

708
00:49:39,589 --> 00:49:41,490
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why virtue and duty is not the dhamma.

709
00:49:43,870 --> 00:49:47,331
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is no dhamma without that in front of you.

710
00:49:47,691 --> 00:49:51,632
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no peripheral of virtue and duty without virtue and duty developed.

711
00:49:56,393 --> 00:50:03,655
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you might ask, why is the dhamma... why is the dhamma the peripheral of virtue and duty and not the peripheral of non-virtue and non-duty?

712
00:50:04,665 --> 00:50:05,587
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just how things are.

713
00:50:08,251 --> 00:50:12,137
[SPEAKER_01]: Or because virtue and duty emulates the principle of dhamma, unknowingly.

714
00:50:12,978 --> 00:50:14,841
[SPEAKER_01]: And non-virtue and non-duty does not.

715
00:50:14,962 --> 00:50:17,566
[SPEAKER_01]: It contradicts the principle of dhamma, unknowingly.

716
00:50:20,389 --> 00:50:23,010
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to start emulating it in order to discern it.

717
00:50:23,450 --> 00:50:26,612
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're not even emulating in front of you, how can you then discern it?

718
00:50:26,952 --> 00:50:29,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're neither doing it unknowingly, non-knowingly.

719
00:50:30,093 --> 00:50:31,114
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just not there.

720
00:50:31,774 --> 00:50:33,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, sense restraint is necessary.

721
00:50:33,895 --> 00:50:35,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Virtue is necessary.

722
00:50:36,276 --> 00:50:39,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they emulate the principle of the correct nature of things.

723
00:50:40,578 --> 00:50:40,838
[SPEAKER_01]: How?

724
00:50:41,038 --> 00:50:43,840
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's your job to discern without losing their basis.

725
00:50:43,860 --> 00:50:46,621
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you start becoming non-virtuous,

726
00:50:47,153 --> 00:50:50,954
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, how can you... Well, peripheral of non-virtue is equally non-virtuous.

727
00:50:51,495 --> 00:50:54,316
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you can't be mindful doing unwholesome things.

728
00:50:55,076 --> 00:50:56,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can think you are.

729
00:51:00,798 --> 00:51:02,378
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you protect your mindfulness.

730
00:51:02,458 --> 00:51:06,820
[SPEAKER_01]: You protect the peripheral by being virtuous and sense restrained.

731
00:51:07,840 --> 00:51:11,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by unmindful, unmindful, unmindful, unmindful, unmindful, unmindful.

732
00:51:11,722 --> 00:51:11,862
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

733
00:51:12,502 --> 00:51:15,123
[SPEAKER_01]: You're mindful by not being non-virtuous and non-dutiful.

734
00:51:16,633 --> 00:51:24,297
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you're mindful.

735
00:51:24,897 --> 00:51:25,758
[SPEAKER_01]: And non-dutiful, yeah.

736
00:51:27,118 --> 00:51:29,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it's your duty to practice sense restraint.

737
00:51:30,180 --> 00:51:31,461
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your duty to not indulge.

738
00:51:31,561 --> 00:51:34,522
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your duty to sort of, you know, inform yourself, read the suttas.

739
00:51:35,123 --> 00:51:35,923
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't do that?

740
00:51:36,203 --> 00:51:39,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, good luck figuring out then the peripheral of the duty.

741
00:51:40,145 --> 00:51:40,845
[SPEAKER_01]: The correct duty.

742
00:51:41,166 --> 00:51:43,747
[SPEAKER_01]: The duty that doesn't contradict the universal principle of dhamma.

743
00:51:46,168 --> 00:51:52,991
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not your duty to, you know, like, do these, like, pointless things that are today perceived as religious duty.

744
00:51:53,091 --> 00:51:54,112
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not talking about that.

745
00:51:54,552 --> 00:51:57,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is usually how people try to dismiss Sīla Bhātta Pāramāsa.

746
00:51:57,173 --> 00:51:58,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, rites and rituals.

747
00:51:58,754 --> 00:51:58,894
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

748
00:51:59,054 --> 00:52:00,455
[SPEAKER_01]: Sīla Bhātta Pāramāsa.

749
00:52:01,355 --> 00:52:05,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Sīla, the virtue and duty, sense of duty of the virtuous one.

750
00:52:06,037 --> 00:52:06,497
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm a monk.

751
00:52:06,597 --> 00:52:07,418
[SPEAKER_01]: This is my duty.

752
00:52:07,618 --> 00:52:10,579
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what talks... That's what the fetter is.

753
00:52:10,599 --> 00:52:12,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Not lighting shrines and whatnot.

754
00:52:13,978 --> 00:52:19,983
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's the obvious kind of rites and rituals, but that's not what the Buddha was talking about.

755
00:52:21,164 --> 00:52:23,085
[SPEAKER_01]: If so, he would have called it rites and rituals.

756
00:52:23,105 --> 00:52:27,989
[SPEAKER_01]: He wouldn't have called it the same word for sīla, which is a necessary ground for dhamma.

757
00:52:30,091 --> 00:52:31,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Sīla bhārata paramāsa.

758
00:52:31,852 --> 00:52:33,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Not some ridiculous sīla.

759
00:52:33,213 --> 00:52:34,234
[SPEAKER_01]: The sīla that you have.

760
00:52:35,675 --> 00:52:36,415
[SPEAKER_01]: The important one.

761
00:52:38,317 --> 00:52:39,478
[SPEAKER_01]: So, peripheral of that.

